.Dd September 28, 2026
.Dt MKNOD 2
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm mknod ,
.Nm mknodat
.Nd make a FIFO, a socket, a device node or an empty file
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In sys/stat.h
.Ft int
.Fn mknod "const char *path" "mode_t mode" "dev_t dev"
.Ft int
.Fn mknodat "int dirfd" "const char *path" "mode_t mode" "dev_t dev"
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Fn mknod
makes the node
.Fa path
of the kind the file type bits of
.Fa mode
give:
.Dv S_IFIFO
a FIFO,
.Dv S_IFSOCK
a socket file,
.Dv S_IFCHR
and
.Dv S_IFBLK
a character or block device node with the number
.Fa dev ,
and
.Dv S_IFREG
or no type bits an empty regular file.
.Fa dev
is ignored for the other kinds.
.Fn mknodat
does the same with a relative
.Fa path
resolved from the directory
.Fa dirfd
is open on, or from the current working directory for
.Dv AT_FDCWD ;
an absolute path ignores
.Fa dirfd .
Musl's
.Fn mkfifo
is
.Fn mknod
with
.Dv S_IFIFO .
.Pp
The node receives the caller's effective user and group IDs and the
permission bits
.Li "mode & 07777 & ~umask" .
Its parent must exist, the caller needs search permission on the
ancestors and write permission on the parent, and the final name must
not exist, whether as a file, a directory, a symbolic link or a mount
point.
A device node needs effective user ID 0.
A path that names a directory by its end, which the node is not, makes
nothing, as on Linux: with a slash after the final name it returns
.Er ENOENT
when nothing has the name and
.Er EEXIST
when something does, and with a last component of
.Pa \&.
or
.Pa \&..
it returns
.Er EEXIST ,
as the root does.
.Pp
.Fa dev
is Linux's 32-bit encoding, which musl's
.Fn makedev
produces in its low word: a major number up to 4095 and a minor number
up to 1048575.
.Xr stat 2
reports it as
.Va st_rdev .
.Pp
A FIFO is opened as an end of a pipe shared by every open of the file,
as
.Xr open 2
describes.
A socket file is made by
.Xr bind 2
on an
.Dv AF_UNIX
socket; one made by
.Fn mknod
has no socket bound to it, and
.Xr connect 2
to it is refused.
An open of a socket file fails with
.Er ENXIO .
An open of a device node opens the
.Pa /dev
device with the same number and kind, as
.Xr open 2
describes.
.Ss File systems
ext2 keeps every kind.
It writes the inode before the directory entry that names it, and keeps
a device's number where Linux does: the major and minor numbers a byte
each in the first block pointer word when both are below 256, and the
32-bit encoding in the second word, with the first zero, otherwise.
The entry's type byte names the kind.
.Pp
tmpfs keeps every kind in memory.
.Pp
KRFFS, the boot root and a mounted volume alike, keeps FIFOs and sockets:
the node's stored mode has their type bits, written before its name.
A KRFFS node has no field for a device's number, so a device node there
is refused with
.Er EPERM .
.Pp
FAT32 and exFAT have no such nodes and refuse every kind but a regular
file with
.Er EPERM .
Nothing is made under
.Pa /dev
or
.Pa /proc ,
which answer
.Er EROFS
after the answers above for a path that names a directory by its end,
and after
.Er EEXIST
for a name that exists there, as on Linux.
.Pp
The i386 system call numbers are 14 for
.Fn mknod
and 297 for
.Fn mknodat .
.Fn mknod
takes the path, the mode and the device number in EBX, ECX and EDX;
.Fn mknodat
takes the directory descriptor, the path, the mode and the device
number in EBX, ECX, EDX and ESI.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
These functions return 0 on success, or -1 on failure and set
.Va errno .
The kernel interface returns the negative error number.
.Sh EXAMPLES
.Pa tests/musl/fifomusl.c
makes a FIFO in
.Pa /tmp
with
.Fn mkfifo
and another with
.Fn mknodat ,
and checks that
.Xr stat 2
reports each as one.
.Sh ERRORS
The errors are checked in this order: the kind, the path, the name's
existence, a read-only mount, the parent's write permission, and a
device node's privilege.
.Bl -tag -width "[ENAMETOOLONG]"
.It Bq Er EACCES
Search permission on an ancestor or write permission on the parent
was denied.
.It Bq Er EBADF
.Fn mknodat
was given a relative path and a
.Fa dirfd
that is not open.
.It Bq Er EBUSY
Path resolution entered a mount being mounted or unmounted.
.It Bq Er EEXIST
The name exists, as any kind of node or as a mount point, the path is
the root, or its last component is
.Pa \&.
or
.Pa \&.. .
.It Bq Er EFAULT
The path is null or cannot be read from user memory, or there is no
current process.
.It Bq Er EINVAL
The type bits of
.Fa mode
are none of the five kinds and not a directory's.
.It Bq Er EIO
The backing device was removed, or file system metadata could not be
read or written.
.It Bq Er ELOOP
Path resolution would follow more than 40 symbolic links.
.It Bq Er ENAMETOOLONG
A path, component or symbolic-link expansion is too long.
.It Bq Er ENOENT
The path is empty, an ancestor is missing, or a slash follows a final
name nothing has.
.It Bq Er ENOSPC
There is no space for the node or its directory entry.
.It Bq Er ENOTDIR
An ancestor is not a directory, or
.Fn mknodat
was given a relative path and a
.Fa dirfd
that is not open on a directory.
.It Bq Er EPERM
.Fa mode
has the type bits of a directory, a device node was asked for by a
caller whose effective user ID is not 0, or the file system cannot
keep the kind: a device node on KRFFS, any kind but a regular file on
FAT32 and exFAT.
.It Bq Er EROFS
The mount is read-only, or the parent is under
.Pa /dev
or
.Pa /proc .
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr bind 2 ,
.Xr mkdir 2 ,
.Xr open 2 ,
.Xr stat 2 ,
.Xr umask 2
